  {
    "title": "York County Republican Lawmakers Issue Statement on North Codorus Township Shooting",
    "url": "https://repmarcanderson.com/News/36720/Press-Releases/York-County-Republican-Lawmakers-Issue-Statement-on-North-Codorus-Township-Shooting",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "York County Republican Lawmakers Issue Statement on North Codorus Township Shooting\nSeptember 19, 2025\nHARRISBURG\n— State Republican lawmakers representing York County issued the following statement following the incident that took place in North Codorus Township, York County, yesterday that left three police officers dead and two injured. The lawmakers consist of Sens. Kristin Phillips-Hill (R-28) and Dawn Keefer (R-31); and Reps. Seth Grove (R-Dover), Kate Klunk (R-Hanover), Mike Jones (R-York Township), Joe D’Orsie (R-Manchester), Wendy Fink (R-Red Lion) and Marc Anderson (R-Dillsburg).\n“We are heartbroken and deeply saddened by the devastating loss of Det. Sgt. Cody Becker, Det. Isaiah Emenheiser and Det. Mark Baker, who were killed in the line of duty, and we extend our heartfelt prayers to the two officers who were injured while courageously serving our community.\n“These officers made the ultimate sacrifice while carrying out their duty — a duty rooted in protecting and upholding the safety of others. We owe them, and every member of law enforcement who risks their life each day, a debt that can never truly be repaid.\n“To the families, friends and colleagues of those we lost: we grieve with you, and we stand with you. No words can ease the pain you are feeling, but please know that the entire community mourns with you, honors their service, and remembers their heroism.\n“As we process this tragedy, we ask our community to keep the fallen officers and the injured in their thoughts and prayers. Let us come together in unity, in gratitude for their service, and in support of the law enforcement community that continues to protect us with unwavering courage.\n“We will never forget their sacrifice.”\nYork County Republican Delegation\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Eight Republican state lawmakers representing York County issued a joint statement expressing condolences following a shooting in North Codorus Township that killed three police detectives and injured two others. The statement honors the fallen officers by name and offers support to their families and the broader law enforcement community. No legislative action was proposed or taken.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "York County House Republicans Advocate to Save Women’s Sports",
    "url": "https://repmarcanderson.com/News/36455/Press-Releases/York-County-House-Republicans-Advocate-to-Save-Women-s-Sports",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "York County House Republicans Advocate to Save Women’s Sports\nJuly 8, 2025\nHARRISBURG— Members of the York County Republican Delegation to the House of Representatives signaled their support for\nSenate Bill 9\n, the Save Women’s Sports Act, following the signing of a discharge resolution that aims to bring the bill to the full House for a vote. The delegation consists of Reps. Seth Grove (R-Dover), Kate Klunk (R-Hanover), Mike Jones (R-York Township), Joe D’Orsie (R-Manchester), Wendy Fink (R-Red Lion) and Marc Anderson (R-Dillsburg).\nUnder Senate Bill 9, school athletic teams designated for female students would be closed to biological males, prohibiting men from competing with women.\nWhile the measure has stalled in the Democrat-controlled House, it passed the state Senate with bipartisan support in May. If adopted by a majority vote in the House, the discharge resolution (\nHouse Resolution 281\n) would require Senate Bill 9 to be brought up for consideration by the full House.\nThe York County Republican Delegation members issued the following statement:\n“Protecting equal opportunities for female athletes is worth fighting for; and as a legislature, we are obligated to protect them from discrimination, potential abuse and physical harm. Allowing biological males to compete on women’s teams puts female athletes at an unfair disadvantage, jeopardizes their safety, and creates barriers to potentially life-changing opportunities, like college scholarships.\n“We are proud to support House Resolution 281, which may leverage a vote on Senate Bill 9, to protect female athletes and fulfill the legal requirements stipulated by Title IX, which was designed to end discrimination and create equal athletic opportunities for women.\n“The delegation is energized to approve this legislation. We urge our Democrat colleagues to stop using procedural maneuvers (as they did today by changing the bill’s committee referral) to further avoid a vote. Pennsylvania’s female athletes deserve protection under the law, and we are ready to assure that protection for current and future generations.”\nYork County House Republicans\nPennsylvania General Assembly\nGrove Media Contact: Greg Gross, 717-260-6374\nKlunk Media Contact: Katelin Morrison, 717-260-6502\nJones Media Contact: Donna Pinkham, 717-260-6452\nD’Orsie Media Contact: Melissa Fox, 717-260-6522\nFink Media Contact: Katelin Morrison, 717-260-6502\nAnderson Media Contact: Tricia Lehman, 717-772-9840\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Six Republican state representatives from York County signed onto a discharge resolution (House Resolution 281) intended to force a floor vote on Senate Bill 9, which would ban transgender girls and women from competing on female school sports teams. The bill passed the state Senate in May but has not advanced in the Democrat-controlled House. The discharge resolution is a procedural tool that, if approved by a majority of the full House, would bypass committee and require the bill to be brought to a floor vote.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "education",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  }
